comments.body:
I took a look into this bug and it seems the type signedness is intentionally discarded during the `MHLOToLinalgOnTensors` pass. This is done because the LLVMs dialect such as linalg, arith, etc uses the operators to convey signedness (ex. `arith.cmpi slt`/`arith.cmpi ult`), while MHLO uses the type (`i32` /`ui32`).

```
// -----// IR Dump After ConvertMHLOToLinalgOnTensors (iree-mhlo-to-linalg-on-tensors) //----- //
#loc = loc(unknown)
module @jit_prim_fun {
  func.func public @main(%arg0: tensor<5x7xi32> loc(unknown)) -> tensor<5x7xi32> {
    %0 = iree_linalg_ext.sort dimension(1) outs(%arg0 : tensor<5x7xi32>) {
    ^bb0(%arg1: i32 loc(unknown), %arg2: i32 loc(unknown)):
      %1 = arith.cmpi ult, %arg1, %arg2 : i32 loc(#loc3)
      iree_linalg_ext.yield %1 : i1 loc(#loc2)
    } -> tensor<5x7xi32> loc(#loc2)
    return %0 : tensor<5x7xi32> loc(#loc)
  } loc(#loc)
} loc(#loc)
#loc1 = loc("/Users/phawkins/p/jax/tests/lax_test.py":2039:1)
#loc2 = loc("jit(sort)/jit(main)/sort[dimension=1 is_stable=False num_keys=1]"(#loc1))
#loc3 = loc("jit(sort)/jit(main)/lt"(#loc1))
```

It seems that we need to retain the orginal type somewhere in the IR in order to fix this. I'm not sure what is the best way to accomplish that. Maybe it could be done using some kind of attribute in the outer module or with a cast op on the output? Or should the MLIR dialects retain the signedness in the type and just ignore it while deferring to the signedness in the operations?

